<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-6-overloading">
  <title>Overloading Methods and Constructors</title>

  <p>
    In Chapter 5, you learned that we can overload methods by writing more than one method 
    with the same name. Overloaded methods must have different parameter lists. Java matches 
    a method call to the appropriate method by matching the number, order, and type of 
    arguments to the parameters.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is also common to overload instance methods, and in particular, to overload the 
    constructor. We overload methods if we want to provide the same operation with different 
    levels of customization, or if we want to provide the same operation with different 
    parameter types that cannot be handled through widening primitive conversions.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-6-1-default-constructor">
    <title>The Default Constructor</title>

    <p>
      When we create an object with the <c>new</c> keyword, Java allocates a block of memory 
      for the object on the heap. That memory consists of space for each of the object's 
      fields. Fields are allocated in a way that is similar to variables on the stack: 
      primitives are stored directly in the object, and if an object has references to other 
      objects, then it is the references that are stored. For example, the <c>ElectricVehicle</c> 
      class has a <c>model</c> field which is a <c>String</c>. Because a <c>String</c> is an 
      object, <c>model</c> is a reference to another location in the heap.
    </p>

    <p>
      When space for an object is first allocated, the memory for the fields is zeroed out. 
      If you forget to give a field an initial value in the constructor, the fields will be 
      assigned the default values shown in <xref ref="table-default-field-values"/>, according 
      to their type. These are simply the values you get when zeroes are stored in the memory 
      location.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-default-field-values">
      <title>Default initial field values</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Data type</cell>
          <cell>Value</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Integer types (<c>long</c>, <c>int</c>, <c>short</c>, <c>byte</c>)</cell>
          <cell><c>0</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Floating-point types (<c>double</c>, <c>float</c>)</cell>
          <cell><c>0.0</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><c>char</c></cell>
          <cell><c>'\u0000'</c> (the null character)</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><c>boolean</c></cell>
          <cell><c>false</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Object reference types</cell>
          <cell><c>null</c> (a special value indicating no reference)</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Always initialize all fields in a constructor, even if you are going to initialize a 
      field to the zeroed-out value it has already been given by the JVM. Otherwise, another 
      programmer reading your code will not know if the missing initialization is intentional 
      or an oversight.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every class in Java has a constructor, whether you write one or not. If you do not 
      write any constructor for a class, Java automatically provides a <em>default constructor</em>. 
      The default constructor takes no arguments and leaves the field values at their default 
      zeroed-out values.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, <xref ref="listing-step-tracker-no-constructor"/> is a <c>StepTracker</c> 
      class and <xref ref="listing-step-tracker-demo"/> is a <c>StepTrackerDemo</c> program. 
      The <c>StepTracker</c> class has no programmer-defined constructor, so Java provides a 
      default constructor, and the values of <c>steps</c> and <c>days</c> are each initialized 
      to <c>0</c> when the object is created on line 5 of <c>StepTrackerDemo</c>.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-step-tracker-no-constructor">
      <caption>A class with no programmer-defined constructor</caption>
      <program language="java">
        <code>
public class StepTracker {
    
    private int steps;
    private int days;
    
    public void addDailySteps(int newSteps) {
        assert newSteps >= 0 : "Steps cannot be negative: " + newSteps;
        this.steps += newSteps;
        this.days++;
    }
    
    public double getAverageSteps() {
        return this.days != 0 ? (double) this.steps / this.days : 0.0;
    }
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <listing xml:id="listing-step-tracker-demo">
      <caption>A program that creates a <c>StepTracker</c>, invoking the default constructor</caption>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="java">
        <code>
public class StepTrackerDemo {
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creates a tracker for the user:
        StepTracker myTracker = new StepTracker();
        
        // Displays the average steps.
        System.out.println(myTracker.getAverageSteps());
        
        // Adds some steps.
        myTracker.addDailySteps(7000);
        
        // Displays the average steps.
        System.out.println(myTracker.getAverageSteps());
    }
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      When line 11 in <c>StepTrackerDemo</c> executes, execution jumps to the <c>addDailySteps</c> 
      method of <c>StepTracker</c> (line 6), and the argument <c>7000</c> is added to the 
      <c>steps</c> field (the default value of <c>0</c> given by the JVM), making it <c>7000</c>. 
      The <c>days</c> field, also initialized to <c>0</c> by the JVM, is incremented to <c>1</c>. 
      The output of <c>StepTrackerDemo</c> is:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
0.0
7000.0
      </output>
    </console>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-6-2-overloading-constructors">
    <title>Overloading Constructors</title>

    <p>
      Suppose you decide that you want to allow a client of the <c>StepTracker</c> class to 
      instantiate an object with existing baseline values for <c>steps</c> and <c>days</c>. 
      You add the following parameterized constructor:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
    public StepTracker(int steps, int days) {
        assert steps >= 0 &amp;&amp; days >= 0 : "Steps and days cannot be negative";
        this.steps = steps;
        this.days = days;
    }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      If you look back at your <c>StepTrackerDemo</c> program, you will now see a compiler 
      error on line 5:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
The constructor StepTracker() is undefined.
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      Java only creates a default constructor if there are <em>no programmer-defined constructors</em>. 
      Once you write any constructor, Java stops providing the automatic default constructor. 
      Any existing code that relied on creating an object with no arguments is now broken.
    </p>

    <p>
      In many cases, you will want to allow users of your class to instantiate an object either 
      in a default state or with custom values. You can overload the constructor to support both.
    </p>

    <p>
      A constructor that takes no parameters is called a <em>no-arg</em> (no-argument) constructor. 
      We can write a no-arg constructor for <c>StepTracker</c>:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
    // Functional, but see below for a better way!
    public StepTracker() {
        this.steps = 0;
        this.days = 0;
    }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Now any code that previously relied on a zero-argument constructor will compile and run. 
      A no-arg constructor does not necessarily have to zero out the fields<mdash/>depending on 
      the class, different default values may be appropriate.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is best practice in Java to <q>chain</q> constructors to eliminate redundancy. Instead 
      of writing the no-arg constructor to assign field values directly, we should call the 
      parameterized constructor and pass in the default values. We use the <c>this</c> keyword 
      followed by parentheses to invoke a constructor of the same class:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
    // Chain constructors with this(...) to avoid redundancy.
    public StepTracker() {
        this(0, 0);
    }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Not only does this avoid duplicate assignment logic, but if you later add validation rules 
      or initial setup logic to the primary constructor, the no-arg constructor inherits that 
      behavior automatically.
    </p>

    <p>
      The explicit constructor invocation (<c>this(0, 0);</c>) <strong>must be the very first 
      statement</strong> in the constructor body. Once that constructor finishes and the object 
      is in a stable initial state, additional statements may execute.
    </p>

    <p>
      One final note regarding constructor parameters: you will notice that we name parameters 
      according to the fields they initialize (the <c>steps</c> parameter initializes the 
      <c>steps</c> field; the <c>days</c> parameter initializes the <c>days</c> field). This is 
      a standard Java convention with two distinct benefits:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          It clarifies exactly which field the parameter value is intended to initialize.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          It saves you from having to invent artificial, awkward names (such as <c>s</c> or 
          <c>initialSteps</c>) for the same concept.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Because a parameter is a local variable with the exact same name as a field, it shadows 
      the field. Prepending the field name with <c>this.</c> resolves the shadowing, explicitly 
      indicating that the left-hand variable belongs to the current heap object.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-6-3-overloading-instance-methods">
    <title>Overloading Instance Methods</title>

    <p>
      Just as we overloaded constructors to provide different ways to initialize an object, 
      we can overload instance methods to provide flexible ways to interact with an object's state.
    </p>

    <p>
      In our original <c>StepTracker</c> class, the <c>addDailySteps</c> method requires an exact 
      integer count of steps:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
    public void addDailySteps(int newSteps) {
        assert newSteps >= 0 : "Steps cannot be negative: " + newSteps;
        this.steps += newSteps;
        this.days++;
    }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      What if a user does not know their exact step count, but knows they walked 3.5 miles with 
      an average stride length of 2.5 feet? Rather than forcing the client programmer to perform 
      unit conversions in <c>main</c> before calling our class, we can provide an overloaded 
      version of <c>addDailySteps</c> that accepts distance and stride length:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
    public void addDailySteps(double milesWalked, double strideLengthFeet) {
        assert milesWalked >= 0.0 &amp;&amp; strideLengthFeet > 0.0 : 
            "Distance and stride length must be positive";

        final double FEET_PER_MILE = 5280.0;
        int calculatedSteps = (int) Math.round((milesWalked * FEET_PER_MILE) 
                                               / strideLengthFeet);

        // Delegate to the primary 1-parameter method
        addDailySteps(calculatedSteps);
    }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Notice how the overloaded method works. Instead of duplicating the state-updating code 
      (<c>this.steps += ...; this.days++;</c>), it converts its inputs into steps and immediately 
      delegates to the primary one-parameter <c>addDailySteps</c> method.
    </p>

    <p>
      This follows a core software engineering principle: <em>Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY)</em>:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>A Single Source of Truth:</strong> The actual task of modifying the object's 
          fields lives in exactly one place (the primary <c>addDailySteps(int)</c> method).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Maintainability:</strong> If you later decide to add validation rules (such as 
          capping daily steps at a reasonable maximum) or log activity to a file, you only have 
          to write that logic once in the primary method. The overloaded convenience method 
          automatically inherits the updated behavior.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      From the perspective of client code in <c>main</c>, the class now provides a flexible, 
      intuitive interface:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
StepTracker tracker = new StepTracker();

// Day 1: Log steps directly from a pedometer
tracker.addDailySteps(8500);

// Day 2: Log steps calculated from a morning walk (2.5 miles, 2.5-foot stride)
tracker.addDailySteps(2.5, 2.5);

System.out.println("Average daily steps: " + tracker.getAverageSteps());
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      When <c>tracker.addDailySteps(8500);</c> runs, Java matches the call to the <c>(int)</c> 
      signature. When <c>tracker.addDailySteps(2.5, 2.5);</c> runs, Java matches the call to the 
      <c>(double, double)</c> signature. The object seamlessly handles both formats while 
      keeping its internal state consistent.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>